<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Prüm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/855">855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Prüm was the arrangement of September 855 by which the dying Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a> divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a> among his three sons at the abbey of Prüm in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a>. Louis II received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with the imperial title, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> the northern lands that became Lotharingia, and Charles Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. Lothair I then entered the monastery, where he died days later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-prum.jpg" alt="Treaty of Prüm" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Furfur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vertrag_von_Pr%C3%BCm.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>From 830 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> and his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-i-of-aquitaine">Pepin I of Aquitaine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> fought a series of dynastic struggles with their father, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>. After the emperor's death in 840, Lothair, co-emperor since 817, claimed supremacy over his surviving brothers Louis the German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, but they defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fontenoy">Battle of Fontenoy</a> in 841 and confirmed their pact by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaths-of-strasbourg">Oaths of Strasbourg</a>.</p><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> of August 843 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> received the lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aare">Aare</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> those west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> the lands between, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the imperial title. This <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a> combined long, exposed borders with poor internal communications, and it suffered both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids in the north and Muslim incursions in the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The settlement of 19 September 855 was the second of the main partition treaties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>. The eldest son, Louis II, received the imperial crown and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>, which then covered only the northern half of the peninsula. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> and the parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a> his father had kept at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, a kingdom later called Lotharingia after him. The youngest, Charles, became King of Provence, with Provence and most of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> except the lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The division did not last. Charles died in 863 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> in 869, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-meerssen">Treaty of Meerssen</a> of 870 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and Lotharingia were shared out between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>. Lotharingia, the shortest-lived of the three kingdoms, was succeeded about a century later by the duchies of Upper and Lower Lorraine, while Charles's lands later grew into the Kingdom of Burgundy, also called the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>. The last effects of the treaty ended when Otto I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 951, and he was crowned emperor in 962. Historians regard the treaty as one of the last major results of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partible-inheritance">partible inheritance</a> before feudalism became the main cause of the breaking up of European states.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Prüm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the <b>Treaty of Prüm</b> in 855 Lothair I left the northern part of Middle Francia, from Frisia through the Low Countries and the Rhineland to the Vosges, to his son Lothair II.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lotharingia">Kingdom of Lotharingia</a></li><li><blockquote>Shortly before his death in 855 Lothair retired to the abbey of Prüm and, by the <b>Treaty of Prüm</b>, divided the kingdom among his three sons: Louis II received Italy, Lothair II the north, which became the Kingdom of Lotharingia, and Charles the Kingdom of Provence.</blockquote><a href="/state/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a></li></ul></section>
